  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Promoting rights based multi-sector</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Bhutan is a source and destination country for men, women, and children vulnerable to forced labor and sex trafficking. While the government of Bhutan has stated its commitment to combating human trafficking, it also acknowledged its efforts were hampered by low capacity and awareness of the crime. The goal of this project is to work cooperatively with eight different central government entities and parts of local government to address deficiencies in Bhutanese legislation, to equip police to better investigate trafficking cases, and improve information sharing to target criminals and corrupt leaders and disrupt human trafficking. The project aims to improve the skills and capacity of the Bhutanese government to investigate and prosecute human trafficking. If successful, the project will lead to an increase in investigations and prosecutions of human trafficking, thereby serving as a deterrent to potential traffickers. While the Government of Bhutan does not have formalized diplomatic relations with the United States, it recognizes the leadership of the United States in combating human trafficking and has actively sought to increase direct bilateral partnership on this issue.</narrative>
  </description>
